\pard\sb100\sa100\qc\par
\pard\sb100\sa100 In order to decrease the duplication of data (and in the process bring ourselves to the Fourth Form of Normalization), we've created a table full of nothing but primary and foriegn keysin \b url_relations\b0 . We've been able to remove the duplicate entries in the \b urls table\b0  by creating the \b url_relations table\b0 . We can now accurately express the relationship that both Joe and Jill are related to each one of , and both of, the urls. So let's see exactly what the Fourth Form Of Normalization entails: \par
\pard\keepn\sb100\sa100\b Fourth Normal Form\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\tx720\b0 1. In a many-to-many relationship, independent entities can not be stored in the same table. \par
\pard\sb100\sa100 Since it only applies to the many-to-many relationship, most developers can rightfully ignore this rule. But it does come in handy in certain situations, such as this one. We've successfully streamlined our \b urls table\b0  to remove duplicate entries and moved the relationships into their own table. \par
Just to give you a practical example, now we can select all of Joe's urls by performing the following SQL call: \par
\b SELECT name, url FROM users, urls, url_relations WHERE url_relations.relatedUserId = 1 AND users.userId = 1 AND urls.urlId = url_relations.relatedUrlId\b0  \par
And if we wanted to loop through everybody's User and Url information, we'd do something like this: \par
\b SELECT name, url FROM users, urls, url_relations WHERE users.userId = url_relations.relatedUserId AND urls.urlId = url_relations.relatedUrlId\b0  \par
\b Fifth Normal Form\b0  \par
There is one more form of normalization which is sometimes applied, but it is indeed very esoteric and is in most cases probably not required to get the most functionality out of your data structure or application. It's tenet suggests: \par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\tx720 1. The original table must be reconstructed from the tables into which it has been broken down. \par
\pard\sb100\sa100 The benefit of applying this rule ensures you have not created any extraneous columns in your tables, and that all of the table structures you have created are only as large as they need to be. It's good practice to apply this rule, but unless you're dealing with a very large data schema you probably won't need it. \par
I hope you have found this article useful, and are able to begin applying these rules of normalization to all of your database projects. And in case you're wondering where all of this came from, the first three rules of normalization were outlined by Dr. E.F. Codd in his 1972 paper, "Further Normalization of the Data Base Relational Model". Other rules have since been theorized by later Set Theory and Relational Algebra mathematicians. \par
--Barry \par
